1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:00,701
Radar.

2
00:00:00,702 --> 00:00:10,000
Abstract blips on a screen that translate into planes, bombs, and surprise attacks.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:10,901
Summer, 1940.

4
00:00:10,902 --> 00:00:17,000
The Nazi blitzkrieg sweeps through Europe and Britain shudders on the verge of collapse.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:24,000
In Liverpool, a lone British physicist has the prototype for England's last hope for survival.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:30,400
Now, Radar, the invention that saved democracy on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,800
May 1940.

8
00:00:59,801 --> 00:01:05,820
Germany crushes French forces, conquering the country in a matter of weeks.

9
00:01:05,821 --> 00:01:09,180
Hitler rides triumphantly into Paris.

10
00:01:09,181 --> 00:01:15,560
Just miles away, 20-year-old Pat Hancock finds his Royal Air Force Squadron squarely in the

11
00:01:15,561 --> 00:01:18,200
teeth of the Nazi advance.

12
00:01:18,201 --> 00:01:26,600
Germans were advancing madly, and from then on, we literally retreated, if not every day, every two or three days.

13
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,820
They had already, of course, swept through Norway before that.

14
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:38,340
Then they occupied Belgium, Denmark, and they were having a great old time.

15
00:01:38,341 --> 00:01:49,820
Hitler is on the verge of completing his lightning conquest of Europe.

16
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:55,260
Only Britain, a tiny island surrounded by the Nazi blitzkrieg, stands in his way.

17
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:00,560
Defying the grim odds, the British refuse to surrender.

18
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:06,820
England was under severe air attack.

19
00:02:06,821 --> 00:02:11,580
And of course, the purpose of this attack was to knock out the Royal Air Force

20
00:02:11,581 --> 00:02:16,260
as a prelude to a cross-channel assault on England itself.

21
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:20,840
And Hitler knew that he had the military strength to move across the channel,

22
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:25,920
but he dared not venture it against the Royal Navy unless he could establish air superiority.

23
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,980
Few doubted that England would soon fall.

24
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:36,740
The German Luftwaffe fleet numbered 2,400 planes, four times that of the RAF.

25
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:39,320
And once Hitler captured England,

26
00:02:39,321 --> 00:02:42,420
his hold on the continent would be secured.

27
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:47,220
Even the United States might be unable to challenge his domination.

28
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:52,080
The fate of freedom in Europe and around the world hung in the balance.

29
00:03:12,451 --> 00:03:17,980
The one hope for British survival was the new and top secret technology of radar.

30
00:03:17,981 --> 00:03:21,740
Radar was more discovered than invented.

31
00:03:21,741 --> 00:03:25,180
By the 1930s, radio waves were everywhere,

32
00:03:25,181 --> 00:03:28,520
conveying entertainment and information to millions.

33
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:30,980
In the course of transmission,

34
00:03:30,981 --> 00:03:33,720
radio technicians noticed that large objects

35
00:03:33,721 --> 00:03:35,260
reflected their signals.

36
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:39,480
The phenomenon was dubbed radar

37
00:03:39,481 --> 00:03:42,600
for radio detecting and ranging.

38
00:03:42,601 --> 00:03:47,340
The word radar is spelled the same forward or backward,

39
00:03:47,341 --> 00:03:50,300
a clever play on the process it describes.

40
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:57,200
When the radar antenna sends out its pulses, they bounce back from the unknown target,

41
00:03:57,201 --> 00:04:00,540
and the result is seen as a peak on the radar screen.

42
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:06,260
Reading this pattern, the distance and speed of an approaching plane are charted.

43
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:16,900
Germany and the United States saw radar as one of many technologies worth pursuing.

44
00:04:16,901 --> 00:04:22,040
but British leaders faced with Hitler's threats gave air defense top priority.

45
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:29,060
Their radar pioneers worked out of this remote Gothic mansion 90 miles from London.

46
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:34,560
They developed a national radar system, the Chain Home Network.

47
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:40,320
By 1940, the eastern coastline of England was ringed with 21 radar towers.

48
00:04:40,321 --> 00:04:47,320
These electronic sentries provided early warning of enemy attacks from up to 100 miles away.

49
00:04:47,321 --> 00:04:53,320
Hitler and his generals had carefully built the most awesome fighting machine ever,

50
00:04:53,321 --> 00:04:57,320
but they were unprepared for this new technology.

51
00:04:57,321 --> 00:05:05,320
Radar was entirely unanticipated, and when the strategists of air power developed their ideas in the 1920s and 1930s,

52
00:05:05,321 --> 00:05:08,920
what they imagined was free access to attack

53
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:14,060
on the enemy's homeland, and radar denied them that.

54
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:16,800
Hermann Goring, chief of the Luftwaffe,

55
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:19,960
suspected that the huge towers bordering England

56
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:22,000
were a radar network.

57
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:24,440
A German zeppelin was dispatched over England

58
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:27,980
to search for radio waves.

59
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:32,140
It detected none, possibly because of scanning

60
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:35,320
the wrong wavelengths.

61
00:05:35,321 --> 00:05:38,880
The chain home radar reached across the English Channel,

62
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:45,160
detecting unsuspecting enemy aircraft as they massed in attack formations over French airfields.

63
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:51,740
Data on the location and speed of the Luftwaffe squadrons went to underground operation centers.

64
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:56,820
Their giant maps of the coastline were marked to reflect the latest intelligence.

65
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:04,920
Generals relayed their orders to four RAF command centers where fighter planes were scrambled to intercept.

66
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:11,460
At this stage, we simply knew that we were able to go.

67
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:16,580
We leapt into our airplanes with the ground crew straggling us in at high speed.

68
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:19,940
And off we went, following the leader.

69
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:21,680
Grass fields, remember, no runway.

70
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:24,300
We simply took up 12 abreast, even 24 abreast sometimes.

71
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:29,580
In World War I, airborne planes were on their own.

72
00:06:29,581 --> 00:06:36,780
But now RAF ground centers maintain control over their squadrons using radar to direct them on intercept courses.

73
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:45,160
Only when enemy planes were spotted would command pass to the pilots who uttered their rallying cry of chally ho.

74
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:48,520
The battle was on.

75
00:06:59,580 --> 00:07:06,460
Radar's early warning allowed the RAF to conserve its limited resources of planes, fuel, and pilots.

76
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:14,220
And by surprising the enemy at the right place with the right number of aircraft,

77
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:18,800
the RAF downed two Luftwaffe planes for every one lost.

78
00:07:18,801 --> 00:07:32,620
as the battle of britain stretched over the summer of 1940 radar denied hitler the air superiority

79
00:07:32,621 --> 00:07:39,480
he needed to launch an invasion for the first time the nazi blitzkrieg had been slowed and

80
00:07:39,481 --> 00:07:45,440
proof that germany was vulnerable gave english civilians the courage to withstand the constant

81
00:07:45,441 --> 00:07:47,360
bombardment of their homes.

82
00:07:55,220 --> 00:08:01,480
During the battle, American physicist George Vallee saw firsthand the resolve of ordinary

83
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:03,480
Londoners to outlast Hitler.

84
00:08:03,481 --> 00:08:09,740
They built up the fronts of the houses, but behind the fronts of the houses was nothing.

85
00:08:09,741 --> 00:08:13,780
and so you could see what looked like a pretty complete city

86
00:08:13,781 --> 00:08:16,800
and it was actually blown to hell.

87
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:20,680
You can keep a stiff up a little bit a hell of a lot better

88
00:08:20,681 --> 00:08:23,300
if you aren't looking at ruins every day.

89
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:27,940
They fixed it to keep the morale of the Londoners high

90
00:08:27,941 --> 00:08:30,060
and the morale was high.

91
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:38,200
Radar had kept Britain alive, but time was running out.

92
00:08:38,201 --> 00:08:45,200
Even at a kill ratio of 2 to 1, the RAF would soon succumb to the far greater resources of the Luftwaffe.

93
00:08:45,201 --> 00:08:53,200
The British were being slowly drained of important resources in planes and even more importantly in pilots.

94
00:08:53,201 --> 00:08:55,039
This was the best and brightest.

95
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:58,200
You could not bring a young man up to training so fast.

96
00:08:58,201 --> 00:09:03,200
And these constant day after day attacks on the British airfields were taking their toll.

97
00:09:03,201 --> 00:09:04,198
their toll.

98
00:09:04,199 --> 00:09:11,820
For Hitler, complete victory was in sight, but his ignorance of radar would sabotage

99
00:09:11,821 --> 00:09:13,526
his dreams of conquest.

100
00:09:13,527 --> 00:09:18,940
He would commit a tactical blunder that rescued Britain and paved the way

101
00:09:18,941 --> 00:09:23,396
for five more years of radar warfare.

102
00:09:23,397 --> 00:09:30,260
In 1939, British dispatchers misread the new radar system

103
00:09:30,261 --> 00:09:34,660
and sent up squadrons of planes to fight non-existent German planes.

104
00:09:34,661 --> 00:09:38,680
In the confusion, several RAF planes were shot down.

105
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:49,140
In August 1940, Churchill ordered the first aerial attacks on German territory.

106
00:09:50,160 --> 00:09:54,460
Although the strikes caused little damage in Berlin, they enraged Hitler.

107
00:09:55,580 --> 00:09:57,840
Already furious at the British for resisting,

108
00:09:57,841 --> 00:10:01,220
he poured his anger into massive attacks on London.

109
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:05,580
Instead of concentrating on knocking out the radar

110
00:10:05,581 --> 00:10:08,740
and knocking out the airfields and knocking out the planes,

111
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:11,980
they finally turned towards retaliatory raids

112
00:10:11,981 --> 00:10:14,720
against London and the civilian population,

113
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:17,800
and that was a waste of their strategic airpower.

114
00:10:18,420 --> 00:10:20,600
If they had not made that mistake,

115
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:22,580
it's not clear that the Royal Air Force

116
00:10:22,581 --> 00:10:24,000
could have prevailed in that battle.

117
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:34,020
The summer-long struggle culminated in an epic air battle on September 15, 1940.

118
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:40,560
By late in the day, the Luftwaffe had lost 60 aircraft, the RAF 26.

119
00:10:42,580 --> 00:10:46,860
Unknown to Germany, Britain had exhausted its reserves of fighter planes.

120
00:10:47,560 --> 00:10:48,760
England was defenseless.

121
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,700
Of this day, Churchill later wrote,

122
00:10:52,701 --> 00:10:54,280
The odds were great.

123
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:56,040
Our margins small.

124
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:57,900
The stakes infinite.

125
00:11:01,180 --> 00:11:05,820
Germany, busy counting its own losses, finally decided to break off the attack.

126
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:10,400
Two days later, Hitler canceled his invasion plans.

127
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:21,260
Guarded by newfound technology and old-fashioned human tenacity, Britain had survived its darkest hour.

128
00:11:21,261 --> 00:11:25,140
Without radar, we would have been as we were in World War I,

129
00:11:25,141 --> 00:11:27,260
basically depending entirely on our eyeballs.

130
00:11:27,261 --> 00:11:32,260
Radar was a fantastic development.

131
00:11:32,261 --> 00:11:35,260
It was one of the major reasons for winning the battle,

132
00:11:35,261 --> 00:11:37,260
apart from the fact that we did.

133
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:47,260
Even as the Battle of Britain raged,

134
00:11:47,261 --> 00:11:50,060
and raged, English scientists turned their thoughts

135
00:11:50,061 --> 00:11:52,840
to long-term survival.

136
00:11:52,841 --> 00:11:54,640
Faced with few options, they decided

137
00:11:54,641 --> 00:11:57,540
to share their most vital military secrets

138
00:11:57,541 --> 00:11:59,940
with the United States.

139
00:11:59,941 --> 00:12:03,040
Only America possessed the scientific and industrial

140
00:12:03,041 --> 00:12:07,720
capacity to support a long war.

141
00:12:07,721 --> 00:12:10,720
Radar pioneer Taffy Bowen collected his country's

142
00:12:10,721 --> 00:12:14,620
scientific bounty in a single black box.

143
00:12:14,621 --> 00:12:19,420
He actually carried it on the underground to the British Ministry of Supply

144
00:12:19,421 --> 00:12:23,060
and then picked it up again in a cab and took it to his hotel.

145
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:28,160
The box was too big to fit in a safe deposit at the hotel,

146
00:12:28,161 --> 00:12:32,600
so he had to sleep with Britain's most precious secrets wedged under his bed.

147
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:37,300
Of all the blueprints, diagrams, and devices in Bowen's trunk,

148
00:12:37,301 --> 00:12:43,340
none mattered more than a small radio transmitter called the cavity magnetron.

149
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:50,620
Existing radars transmitted at low frequencies or long wavelengths,

150
00:12:50,621 --> 00:12:54,620
with each radio wave measuring 1 to 10 meters.

151
00:12:54,621 --> 00:13:00,620
The cavity magnetron could generate shorter or microwaves only 10 centimeters long.

152
00:13:00,621 --> 00:13:07,620
Microwaves produced a narrower beam much more accurate at pinpointing a target's location.

153
00:13:07,621 --> 00:13:11,620
And while previous systems required large transmitters,

154
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:17,200
Microwaves offered the promise of sophisticated radars compact enough to fit on a plane.

155
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:26,740
The prospect of airborne radar took on added urgency when Hitler switched to night raids on English cities.

156
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:33,980
Thousands took to sleeping in the underground.

157
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:40,460
On a single October night, London was pounded for nine straight hours, leaving hundreds dead.

158
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:54,240
Aboard the Canadian liner, Duchess of Richmond, Taffy Bowen carried the precious cavity magnetron

159
00:13:54,241 --> 00:13:55,400
to the United States.

160
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:03,700
America's first radar research center was hastily established on the MIT campus in Cambridge,

161
00:14:03,701 --> 00:14:09,080
Massachusetts, and named the Radiation Laboratory, or Rad Lab.

162
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:19,360
The nation's top physicists were recruited in secret to this radar project more than a year before the United States even entered the war.

163
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:27,460
Among them were ten future Nobel laureates, several university presidents, two science advisors, two presidents.

164
00:14:27,461 --> 00:14:38,680
The best of the best were corralled in this secret project, often with nothing more than someone they knew telling them to come to Boston to do secret war work.

165
00:14:41,620 --> 00:14:48,620
One of the first recruits was Norman Ramsey, a young physicist and future Nobel Prize winner.

166
00:14:48,621 --> 00:14:54,620
He was summoned to MIT for a temporary stay that would last more than half a century.

167
00:14:54,621 --> 00:15:01,620
It was a very exciting time. The war was, in one sense, had been very depressing,

168
00:15:01,621 --> 00:15:04,620
and at least we were doing something actively about it.

169
00:15:04,621 --> 00:15:09,620
It was apparent that radar was a very major component.

170
00:15:09,621 --> 00:15:16,620
American scientists were new to war work and untutored in the ways of secrecy.

171
00:15:16,621 --> 00:15:17,545
Concerned, Dr.

172
00:15:17,546 --> 00:15:23,620
Ramsey tested the lab's security by having his wife phone and request technical information.

173
00:15:23,621 --> 00:15:29,620
At that time, there was no distinction between internal calls and external calls at the lab.

174
00:15:29,621 --> 00:15:34,620
You could call on external telephone and begin asking questions.

175
00:15:34,621 --> 00:15:36,620
You know, you got very good answers.

176
00:15:36,621 --> 00:15:38,620
It soon became more and more revealing.

177
00:15:38,621 --> 00:15:41,620
And with each bit of information she figured out

178
00:15:41,621 --> 00:15:43,620
was radio location we were working on.

179
00:15:43,621 --> 00:15:47,620
It was figured out it was 10 centimeter wavelength.

180
00:15:47,621 --> 00:15:50,620
She got a tremendous amount of information just by telephone.

181
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:55,620
The Nazis apparently never took advantage

182
00:15:55,621 --> 00:15:57,620
of the lack of security.

183
00:15:57,621 --> 00:16:01,620
But by late 1940, they had belatedly realized

184
00:16:01,621 --> 00:16:05,620
radar's importance and geared up their best scientific minds

185
00:16:05,621 --> 00:16:08,920
minds in a race to match allied technology.

186
00:16:08,921 --> 00:16:12,220
But an unprecedented series of partnerships

187
00:16:12,221 --> 00:16:14,692
would keep the Rad Lab a step ahead.

188
00:16:14,693 --> 00:16:18,400
Physicists accustomed to working alone joined in teams

189
00:16:18,401 --> 00:16:19,575
to develop ideas.

190
00:16:19,576 --> 00:16:24,620
Scientific and military leaders who had clashed in World War I cooperated

191
00:16:24,621 --> 00:16:25,321
freely.

192
00:16:25,322 --> 00:16:32,160
And perhaps most importantly, American physicists delighted in collaborating with

193
00:16:32,161 --> 00:16:35,560
of their English cousins.

194
00:16:35,561 --> 00:16:38,860
Taffy Bowen came to America, and he came to be

195
00:16:38,861 --> 00:16:42,100
the living embodiment of the spirit of the Rad Lab.

196
00:16:42,101 --> 00:16:45,480
Here was this British scientist, the same age

197
00:16:45,481 --> 00:16:47,880
as all the Americans, yet he'd already worked

198
00:16:47,881 --> 00:16:52,620
for five years to protect his country from invasion.

199
00:16:52,621 --> 00:16:55,720
The Rad Lab expanded with astonishing speed.

200
00:16:55,721 --> 00:16:59,580
By war's end, it had over 4,000 workers.

201
00:16:59,581 --> 00:17:02,120
Building after building was turned over to the lab.

202
00:17:03,660 --> 00:17:06,720
And strange domes appeared on MIT rooftops.

203
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:16,300
Inside the mysterious structures, experimental radars showed their stuff by bouncing test signals off the Boston skyline.

204
00:17:16,301 --> 00:17:25,060
During that period of time, the MIT Humor magazine came out with a cartoon showing the

205
00:17:25,061 --> 00:17:32,360
side of MIT with a most fantastic array of structures up on top, which was sort of ridiculing

206
00:17:32,361 --> 00:17:34,460
the amount that we had put up there.

207
00:17:34,461 --> 00:17:37,420
And this was a very conspicuous thing.

208
00:17:37,421 --> 00:17:44,440
Despite this effort, adopting microwave radar for airborne intercept took time.

209
00:17:44,441 --> 00:17:46,940
In Britain, the Luftwaffe's reign of terror

210
00:17:46,941 --> 00:17:51,980
continued unabated into the spring of 1941.

211
00:17:51,981 --> 00:17:55,600
Frustrated in his latest attempt to crush British resolve,

212
00:17:55,601 --> 00:18:03,000
in June 1941, Hitler launched a surprise invasion of Russia.

213
00:18:03,001 --> 00:18:05,400
At the Rad Lab, scientists turned their attention

214
00:18:05,401 --> 00:18:07,960
to developing air-to-ground radar for hunting

215
00:18:07,961 --> 00:18:10,660
ships and submarines.

216
00:18:10,661 --> 00:18:14,560
Soon, no vessel would escape radar's long reach,

217
00:18:14,561 --> 00:18:19,060
not even Germany's most feared warship, the mighty Bismarck.

218
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:26,780
Radar units were installed in some British planes

219
00:18:26,781 --> 00:18:30,040
as early as 1939, but were removed

220
00:18:30,041 --> 00:18:31,840
before they flew missions over Europe

221
00:18:31,841 --> 00:18:35,760
to prevent radar technology from falling into German hands.

222
00:18:35,761 --> 00:18:44,560
Having failed to bomb the British into surrender, Germany was determined to starve them into submission.

223
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:50,280
As an island nation, England was utterly dependent on outside supplies.

224
00:18:51,180 --> 00:18:55,440
Great convoys tried to deliver desperately needed food and fuel,

225
00:18:56,120 --> 00:19:01,320
but the Nazi warship Bismarck terrorized Allied shipping in the North Atlantic.

226
00:19:01,321 --> 00:19:06,860
It sank Britain's most powerful ship, the Hood, in a matter of minutes.

227
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:15,480
When a magazine exploded aboard the Hood, only three survived out of a crew of 1,400 men.

228
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:24,820
Stunned by the loss of their flagship, English morale plummeted.

229
00:19:25,680 --> 00:19:27,560
The Bismarck seemed invincible.

230
00:19:27,561 --> 00:19:34,520
But the Allies struck back, employing their superior radar to haunt the now legendary ship.

231
00:19:35,980 --> 00:19:44,360
After sinking a supply vessel on May 26, 1941, the Bismarck disappeared into a veil of fog and clouds.

232
00:19:44,880 --> 00:19:51,720
A cat-and-mouse chase ensued as the British carrier Ark Royal used its radar to lead a large force in pursuit.

233
00:19:51,721 --> 00:20:00,600
For 30 hours, Allied radar tracked the Bismarck on a zigzag course across the North Atlantic.

234
00:20:07,240 --> 00:20:13,480
When the weather finally cleared, a massive 24-hour assault destroyed the Bismarck.

235
00:20:21,720 --> 00:20:27,200
The radar war was in full swing.

236
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,460
The sides were literally racing with each other

237
00:20:30,461 --> 00:20:32,120
for the newest innovations

238
00:20:32,121 --> 00:20:34,780
and also the newest countermeasures

239
00:20:34,781 --> 00:20:36,280
against the enemy's innovations

240
00:20:36,281 --> 00:20:38,760
and finally counter-countermeasures

241
00:20:38,761 --> 00:20:42,940
until whole scientific teams were devoted to anticipating

242
00:20:42,941 --> 00:20:45,780
what the enemy was likely to do with new technology

243
00:20:45,781 --> 00:20:48,640
and trying to protect themselves against the consequences.

244
00:20:48,641 --> 00:20:50,840
It was a real hothouse atmosphere.

245
00:20:51,720 --> 00:20:54,940
With the United States still officially at peace,

246
00:20:54,941 --> 00:20:59,340
its naval officers were slow to realize radar's importance.

247
00:21:00,580 --> 00:21:04,040
All that would change on December 7, 1941.

248
00:21:05,720 --> 00:21:08,880
At 7 o'clock a.m., radar sites at Pearl Harbor

249
00:21:08,881 --> 00:21:11,680
detected a massive incoming airborne force.

250
00:21:12,640 --> 00:21:15,360
The information was passed up the chain of command,

251
00:21:16,100 --> 00:21:18,140
where it was promptly ignored.

252
00:21:18,141 --> 00:21:21,740
This strange new gadget called radar,

253
00:21:21,780 --> 00:21:24,540
it was decided must be malfunctioning.

254
00:21:24,580 --> 00:21:27,140
At dawn, the Japanese struck.

255
00:21:36,700 --> 00:21:41,500
The surprise assault on Pearl Harbor left over 2,400 dead,

256
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:43,860
300 planes destroyed,

257
00:21:43,900 --> 00:21:47,060
and the entire US Pacific fleet in chaos,

258
00:21:47,061 --> 00:21:51,120
chaos. Never again would radar's early warning power be ignored.

259
00:21:52,280 --> 00:21:57,760
The Pearl Harbor attack, for all the tremendous tragedy it brought, did more to ensure radar's

260
00:21:57,761 --> 00:21:59,996
place in World War II than anything else.

261
00:21:59,997 --> 00:22:02,940
Once it became known that a radar station had actually

262
00:22:02,941 --> 00:22:09,700
spotted the beginnings of the Japanese attack and followed it all the way into Oahu, the Army and

263
00:22:09,701 --> 00:22:15,200
Navy immediately launched these massive programs to assess the state of their radar development.

264
00:22:15,201 --> 00:22:18,460
The war had become truly global.

265
00:22:20,540 --> 00:22:25,580
Allied leaders agreed that their top priority was maintaining supply lines to England.

266
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:32,540
If Hitler starved England into submission, there would be no base from which to liberate Europe.

267
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:38,160
Even the United States might be forced to make a negotiated peace with the Nazis.

268
00:22:38,161 --> 00:22:41,960
We'll win through to absolute victory.

269
00:22:41,961 --> 00:22:48,540
Right after the U.S. entered the war formally in December 1941,

270
00:22:49,060 --> 00:22:52,640
the German Admiral Donitz launched Operation Palkenschlag,

271
00:22:52,641 --> 00:22:57,340
the role of the drum, in which he began through his U-boats attacking the shipping

272
00:22:57,341 --> 00:23:01,140
along the east coast of North America and the United States.

273
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:05,120
And this, from the start, totally devastated shipping.

274
00:23:05,260 --> 00:23:07,320
The United States was completely ineffective.

275
00:23:07,460 --> 00:23:09,660
It had no grand plan to protect the ships.

276
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:10,800
There were no convoys.

277
00:23:10,801 --> 00:23:15,000
There were something like nine aircraft able to go out and patrol.

278
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:19,700
The Navy sent its first destroyer out, and it was promptly sunk.

279
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:36,020
The submarines were really pretty bad things.

280
00:23:36,021 --> 00:23:42,340
You could sit out on the corner of Cape Cod and watch a submarine every day.

281
00:23:42,820 --> 00:23:47,640
They'd come up and knock some damn tramp steamer off.

282
00:23:48,420 --> 00:23:50,140
It was just a standard show.

283
00:23:54,000 --> 00:24:00,380
By early 1943, Allied losses in the North Atlantic had reached catastrophic proportions.

284
00:24:00,381 --> 00:24:06,380
In March, 21 supply vessels were sunk at the cost of a lone U-boat.

285
00:24:06,381 --> 00:24:12,380
Thanks to Operation Pockenschlag, Germany was dangerously close to winning the war.

286
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:26,380
Building on microwave technology, the Rad Lab developed air-to-ground radar

287
00:24:26,381 --> 00:24:32,380
precise enough to detect U-boats and compact enough to squeeze into aircraft.

288
00:24:32,381 --> 00:24:36,380
Fitting radars into the airplanes that were already built was a real problem,

289
00:24:36,381 --> 00:24:40,380
but we had one great advantage in the case of the Navy planes.

290
00:24:40,381 --> 00:24:45,380
The larger airplanes had both an officer's head and an enlisted man's head.

291
00:24:45,381 --> 00:24:51,380
That is, separate toilets for the officers and the enlisted men.

292
00:24:51,381 --> 00:24:55,740
and we usually appropriated one or another of these heads

293
00:24:55,741 --> 00:24:57,420
and forced them to use a common one.

294
00:24:58,580 --> 00:25:03,220
The U-boat's primary defense was their presumed invisibility.

295
00:25:04,500 --> 00:25:07,400
Once airborne radar stripped away their veil,

296
00:25:07,960 --> 00:25:09,440
the subs were easy prey.

297
00:25:10,300 --> 00:25:14,280
Radar gave them the option to move against the submarine offensively,

298
00:25:14,340 --> 00:25:17,760
that is, to seek out the submarine and to find it

299
00:25:17,761 --> 00:25:20,480
even when it wasn't moving in on a convoy vessel.

300
00:25:21,380 --> 00:25:24,340
These submarines had to spend a considerable amount of time

301
00:25:24,341 --> 00:25:26,420
on the surface recharging their batteries.

302
00:25:26,421 --> 00:25:28,620
They needed the oxygen to do that.

303
00:25:28,621 --> 00:25:31,140
And when they were on the surface, night or day,

304
00:25:31,141 --> 00:25:35,540
they were vulnerable to detection by radar.

305
00:25:35,541 --> 00:25:39,220
Within a few short months, in the summer of 1943,

306
00:25:39,221 --> 00:25:42,820
the Germans saw victory slip from their grasp.

307
00:25:42,821 --> 00:25:46,320
The battle in the North Atlantic turned against them,

308
00:25:46,321 --> 00:25:49,940
and with it went their chance for global supremacy.

309
00:25:49,941 --> 00:25:53,280
U-boat losses grew steadily, freeing the lifeline to Britain.

310
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:00,660
On May 15, 1943, Admiral Donitz broadcast a plaintive message to his forces at sea.

311
00:26:02,220 --> 00:26:07,720
The enemy, in his efforts to deprive the U-boat of its invisibility,

312
00:26:08,560 --> 00:26:14,900
has developed a system of radio location which puts him several lengths ahead of us.

313
00:26:14,901 --> 00:26:22,240
I am fully aware of all the difficulties you encounter in fighting the convoy defense forces.

314
00:26:22,900 --> 00:26:31,200
As your commander-in-chief, I have done and will continue to do everything to change this situation as soon as possible.

315
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:38,280
German scientists did their best to fight back.

316
00:26:38,281 --> 00:26:44,420
They equipped their subs with a device called Metox that sounded when radio waves reflected off the hall.

317
00:26:44,900 --> 00:26:46,900
signaling the ship to dive to safety.

318
00:26:46,901 --> 00:26:53,000
But the rad lab quickly bested this countermeasure with a counter countermeasure,

319
00:26:54,140 --> 00:26:58,100
radars that transmitted wavelengths too short to be read by Metox.

320
00:27:03,580 --> 00:27:06,740
Having twice survived death blows from the Third Reich,

321
00:27:07,340 --> 00:27:09,680
England and its allies were ready to take the offensive.

322
00:27:09,681 --> 00:27:16,680
Liberating Europe would require the largest and most complicated military operation in history.

323
00:27:16,681 --> 00:27:20,680
The radar war would reach its zenith as scientists on both sides

324
00:27:20,681 --> 00:27:26,680
race to develop the next and decisive generation of technology.

325
00:27:31,680 --> 00:27:36,680
The radar scope in airplanes can detect storm clouds up to 150 miles away.

326
00:27:36,681 --> 00:27:37,381
way.

327
00:27:37,382 --> 00:27:42,660
This is why the fastened seatbelt sign is often turned on several minutes before the

328
00:27:42,661 --> 00:27:44,567
turbulence hits.

329
00:27:44,568 --> 00:27:54,100
The demands of war drove radar research at a fantastic pace, compressing decades

330
00:27:54,101 --> 00:27:57,132
of basic advances into just five years.

331
00:27:57,133 --> 00:28:01,020
At the Rad Lab, physicists knew that only constant

332
00:28:01,021 --> 00:28:03,960
innovation would keep them ahead of their Axis counterparts.

333
00:28:06,440 --> 00:28:10,980
I was asked to head a new group, which was called the Advanced Development Group.

334
00:28:11,280 --> 00:28:17,700
And that group had the assignment of, we used jokingly to say, fighting World War III.

335
00:28:18,180 --> 00:28:20,400
Namely, we were working on future things.

336
00:28:21,140 --> 00:28:25,980
Actually, it turned out the things that we worked on were very much applied during World War II.

337
00:28:25,981 --> 00:28:33,280
One of the toughest challenges was directing planes to targets thousands of miles distant.

338
00:28:34,340 --> 00:28:39,180
In 1943, the Allies began massive airstrikes inside Germany.

339
00:28:39,820 --> 00:28:44,520
But the skies there were cloudy three out of five days, blinding the bombers.

340
00:28:45,820 --> 00:28:46,520
Dr.

341
00:28:46,521 --> 00:28:51,700
George Vallee experimented with converting anti-sub radars into directional beacons.

342
00:28:51,701 --> 00:28:56,700
His new system, the H2X, quickly became a hot property.

343
00:28:57,700 --> 00:29:01,700
And then all of a sudden, out of nowhere,

344
00:29:01,701 --> 00:29:06,700
there arrived Colonel Cowart and another colonel,

345
00:29:06,701 --> 00:29:12,700
and they said, how soon can you equip 12 B-17s?

346
00:29:12,701 --> 00:29:14,700
And I said, huh?

347
00:29:14,701 --> 00:29:19,700
And they said, how soon, doctor, can you equip 12 B-17s?

348
00:29:19,701 --> 00:29:22,200
Well, I had one set.

349
00:29:22,260 --> 00:29:26,400
The H2X, though only accurate to within a few hundred yards,

350
00:29:26,480 --> 00:29:28,640
got the job done.

351
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:31,220
It allowed bombing through clouds and darkness,

352
00:29:31,280 --> 00:29:34,120
peeling away Germany's natural defenses.

353
00:29:43,220 --> 00:29:46,100
The H2X was installed in a select group

354
00:29:46,160 --> 00:29:49,300
of American-made B-17s that acted as a weapon

355
00:29:49,301 --> 00:29:52,000
as aerial pathfinders.

356
00:29:52,001 --> 00:29:56,600
One radar-equipped craft would lead up to 750 B-17s

357
00:29:56,601 --> 00:29:58,900
on bombing raids.

358
00:29:58,901 --> 00:30:01,940
When the leader dropped his bombs, the rest followed suit,

359
00:30:01,941 --> 00:30:05,440
leaving a swath of destruction larger than a square mile.

360
00:30:15,700 --> 00:30:18,260
Now, able to fly under all conditions,

361
00:30:18,261 --> 00:30:23,260
Allied planes doubled their sorties, ravaging Germany.

362
00:30:23,261 --> 00:30:27,100
Just as the war created and unleashed weapons unthinkable

363
00:30:27,101 --> 00:30:29,940
when it began, it revolutionized the concept

364
00:30:29,941 --> 00:30:33,540
of acceptable military tactics.

365
00:30:33,541 --> 00:30:35,840
The advent of area bombing led directly

366
00:30:35,841 --> 00:30:39,540
to the intentional destruction of civilian populations,

367
00:30:39,541 --> 00:30:41,680
a policy which the Allies had insisted

368
00:30:41,681 --> 00:30:43,080
they would never sink to.

369
00:30:43,081 --> 00:30:53,140
And that's when we started to get the targeting of cities in Germany and later in Japan.

370
00:30:53,141 --> 00:31:00,900
In the case of the British, they actually pursued a policy that they called de-housing.

371
00:31:01,280 --> 00:31:07,560
By this, they meant that they would use strategic bombing to eliminate the housing of the industrial workers,

372
00:31:07,561 --> 00:31:09,580
and by dislocating the workers,

373
00:31:09,800 --> 00:31:12,840
they would interfere with the industrial productivity

374
00:31:12,841 --> 00:31:15,540
and therefore the warfighting capability of the German state.

375
00:31:15,600 --> 00:31:19,880
But of course, this was all a euphemism for attacking civilians.

376
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:25,400
Germany attempted to use its radar

377
00:31:25,401 --> 00:31:28,280
against the invading planes with little success.

378
00:31:29,280 --> 00:31:32,660
Starting in 1943, to counter ground-to-air radar,

379
00:31:32,661 --> 00:31:36,860
Allied aircraft dropped bundles of aluminum strips known as chaff.

380
00:31:37,560 --> 00:31:44,440
When these were dropped in front of a radar image, they tended to scatter the image and send back a false image.

381
00:31:44,441 --> 00:31:52,640
And so attacking aircraft would use these as a countermeasure against enemies' defensive radar, and it was enormously effective.

382
00:31:52,880 --> 00:31:59,040
And in fact, a large part of the German countryside ended up being covered with aluminum foil by the time the war ended.

383
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:06,220
In the field of radar, they have the geniuses and we have the ninklepoops.

384
00:32:06,221 --> 00:32:09,200
I hate the rogues like the plague

385
00:32:09,201 --> 00:32:14,180
But in one respect I am obliged to doff my cup to them

386
00:32:14,181 --> 00:32:16,420
After the war's over

387
00:32:16,421 --> 00:32:19,700
I'm going to buy myself a British radio set

388
00:32:19,701 --> 00:32:24,520
As a token of my regard for their high-frequency work

389
00:32:24,521 --> 00:32:28,180
Reich Chancellor Hermann Göring

390
00:32:36,220 --> 00:32:42,700
By pounding German cities relentlessly,

391
00:32:42,701 --> 00:32:47,680
American bombers drew the Luftwaffe into the skies in self-defense.

392
00:32:48,460 --> 00:32:51,500
This led to the total destruction of German airpower,

393
00:32:52,160 --> 00:32:54,380
paving the way for the liberation of the continent.

394
00:32:55,300 --> 00:33:01,200
D-Day invasion was met by zero Luftwaffe airplanes.

395
00:33:01,640 --> 00:33:05,880
Had there been thousands of Luftwaffe airplanes on D-Day,

396
00:33:06,220 --> 00:33:08,120
there would have been strafing,

397
00:33:08,220 --> 00:33:11,760
and our soldiers would have only been able to land

398
00:33:11,860 --> 00:33:14,000
with armor plate over their heads,

399
00:33:14,100 --> 00:33:17,140
and that would have really slowed down the proposition

400
00:33:17,240 --> 00:33:20,200
and maybe caused it to fail.

401
00:33:20,300 --> 00:33:23,540
The D-Day invasion of France became the culmination

402
00:33:23,640 --> 00:33:27,840
of an elaborate electronic chess match.

403
00:33:27,940 --> 00:33:30,140
During the Battle of Britain, defensive radar

404
00:33:30,240 --> 00:33:33,640
had allowed England to husband its limited resources.

405
00:33:33,641 --> 00:33:37,520
Now, ironically, Germany was in the same position,

406
00:33:37,960 --> 00:33:40,240
hoping that radar would protect its frontiers

407
00:33:40,241 --> 00:33:42,720
against a vastly more powerful foe.

408
00:33:43,940 --> 00:33:47,200
The Nazis' only chance to repel the assault

409
00:33:47,201 --> 00:33:49,280
was to concentrate their defenses.

410
00:33:52,060 --> 00:33:54,540
Hitler and his generals would have to guess

411
00:33:54,541 --> 00:33:56,320
where the invasion force would land.

412
00:33:59,620 --> 00:34:01,860
Tired of losing the radar war,

413
00:34:01,861 --> 00:34:07,540
Germany had assembled 600 radars at 200 sites along the coast from Denmark to Spain.

414
00:34:09,780 --> 00:34:16,100
Courageous Allied airmen embarked on a series of low-altitude, high-risk missions to bomb the radars.

415
00:34:17,540 --> 00:34:23,120
All but a few were destroyed, and those still operational were intentionally left intact

416
00:34:23,121 --> 00:34:25,640
to see what the Allies wanted them to see.

417
00:34:25,641 --> 00:34:35,960
In the pre-dawn darkness of June 6, 1944, 1,200 combat vessels and 4,000 landing craft

418
00:34:35,961 --> 00:34:38,460
converged on the beaches of Normandy.

419
00:34:45,880 --> 00:34:50,740
Meanwhile, two phantom invasion forces headed north of the Seine River.

420
00:34:50,741 --> 00:34:55,180
Codename Glimmer and Taxable, these small groups of ships

421
00:34:55,280 --> 00:35:00,520
carried a series of nine-foot radar-reflecting dishes,

422
00:35:00,620 --> 00:35:05,420
each dish mimicking the echo of a 10,000-ton ship.

423
00:35:05,520 --> 00:35:08,820
Above the phony fleets, planes dropped barrels of chaff

424
00:35:08,920 --> 00:35:12,800
as if supporting an actual invasion.

425
00:35:12,900 --> 00:35:17,500
They flew in giant rectangular patterns four by 16 miles

426
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:20,740
and proceeded east at eight knots.

427
00:35:20,741 --> 00:35:25,740
the size and speed of a giant seaborne force.

428
00:35:25,840 --> 00:35:27,740
The deception worked.

429
00:35:27,840 --> 00:35:30,640
Glimmer caused a full-scale alert in Dunkirk,

430
00:35:30,740 --> 00:35:32,180
freezing German troops there.

431
00:35:39,300 --> 00:35:42,400
By the time the actual invasion force landed at Normandy,

432
00:35:42,500 --> 00:35:45,700
the Nazis were so confused, they thought this, too,

433
00:35:45,800 --> 00:35:47,900
was another decoy.

434
00:35:48,000 --> 00:35:49,940
Not until the afternoon of the 6th

435
00:35:49,941 --> 00:35:52,880
that they commit their full defenses to Normandy.

436
00:35:52,980 --> 00:35:55,140
By then, it was too late.

437
00:35:55,240 --> 00:35:58,580
Before nightfall, 150,000 Allied troops

438
00:35:58,680 --> 00:36:00,880
had landed and secured the beachheads.

439
00:36:09,300 --> 00:36:12,200
Either on the high seas and land battles

440
00:36:12,300 --> 00:36:14,960
and the bombing campaign, time and time again,

441
00:36:15,060 --> 00:36:17,960
against a highly effective and resourceful enemy,

442
00:36:17,961 --> 00:36:21,520
It was radar that provided the critical margin of victory.

443
00:36:28,740 --> 00:36:31,580
Within a year of D-Day, Hitler would be dead

444
00:36:31,581 --> 00:36:33,940
and his vaunted Third Reich in ruins.

445
00:36:36,140 --> 00:36:39,780
In Boston, the men and women who had done so much to defeat him

446
00:36:39,781 --> 00:36:42,840
faced the end of the Rad Lab with mixed emotions.

447
00:36:42,841 --> 00:36:52,480
by war's end the rad lab had designed 120 different radar systems american industry had

448
00:36:52,481 --> 00:36:59,000
churned out over 1 million sets with the dropping of the first atomic bomb public attention turned

449
00:36:59,001 --> 00:37:06,360
to los alamos denying the radar wizards their just due but insiders knew well the truth of a

450
00:37:06,361 --> 00:37:13,360
favorite rad lab saying, the atomic bomb ended the war. Radar won it.

451
00:37:20,360 --> 00:37:23,812
As radar units get smaller, their usefulness increases.

452
00:37:23,813 --> 00:37:25,820
Miniature radar units can now be

453
00:37:25,821 --> 00:37:29,549
installed in soldiers' helmets to detect enemy movement.

454
00:37:29,550 --> 00:37:31,680
And tiny radar systems are being

455
00:37:31,681 --> 00:37:33,340
used in canes for the blind.

456
00:37:40,020 --> 00:37:42,560
Even in the darkest days of World War II,

457
00:37:42,561 --> 00:37:45,020
Americans never faced the threat of attacks

458
00:37:45,021 --> 00:37:47,920
on their homes and families.

459
00:37:47,921 --> 00:37:51,800
In 1949, that sense of security would vanish forever.

460
00:37:51,801 --> 00:38:01,340
ever.

461
00:38:01,341 --> 00:38:05,880
In August 1949, American sensors detected unusual amounts

462
00:38:05,881 --> 00:38:09,380
of radioactive dust in the atmosphere.

463
00:38:09,381 --> 00:38:12,280
An analysis confirmed the unthinkable.

464
00:38:12,281 --> 00:38:16,100
Russia had exploded its first nuclear device.

465
00:38:16,101 --> 00:38:18,900
For many in the free world, the thought of Stalin with the bomb

466
00:38:18,901 --> 00:38:21,940
posed an even more terrifying threat than Hitler had.

467
00:38:22,800 --> 00:38:26,980
And this struck everybody with a blow in the chest.

468
00:38:27,960 --> 00:38:30,540
Nobody believed the Russians were smart enough

469
00:38:30,541 --> 00:38:34,400
to learn how to make strike anywhere matches,

470
00:38:34,720 --> 00:38:36,920
much less atom bombs.

471
00:38:38,060 --> 00:38:42,680
The Atlantic and Pacific no longer shielded America from foreign attack.

472
00:38:43,480 --> 00:38:46,220
Other international upheavals quickly followed.

473
00:38:46,221 --> 00:38:53,220
On October 1, 1949, Mao Zedong proclaimed the People's Republic of China.

474
00:38:57,220 --> 00:39:03,220
The following year, the United States and Russia faced off through their client states in South and North Korea.

475
00:39:05,220 --> 00:39:13,220
In December 1950, President Truman responded to Chinese involvement in the war by declaring a national emergency.

476
00:39:13,221 --> 00:39:19,980
I believe that we must try to limit the war to Korea, to see that the security of our

477
00:39:19,981 --> 00:39:27,140
country and the free world is not needlessly jeopardized, and to prevent a third world

478
00:39:27,141 --> 00:39:27,841
war.

479
00:39:29,880 --> 00:39:34,680
All of a sudden, with the bomb in Stalin's hands, America was a vulnerable island, just

480
00:39:34,681 --> 00:39:38,540
as Britain had been back in 1940, facing Hitler's war machine.

481
00:39:38,541 --> 00:39:48,320
This propelled the radar veterans from World War II back into the floor in this furious drive to create a new warning network for the United States.

482
00:39:49,520 --> 00:39:56,720
In the days before satellites, radar offered the only hope of early detection of enemy bombs.

483
00:39:57,640 --> 00:40:01,400
Called SAGE for Semi-Automatic Ground Environment,

484
00:40:01,401 --> 00:40:08,440
the new defense network sought to link massive amounts of information from radar arrays spanning the continent.

485
00:40:08,540 --> 00:40:18,180
SAGE depended on digital computers doing real-time calculations,

486
00:40:18,540 --> 00:40:22,100
allowing information to be collected, collated, analyzed,

487
00:40:22,220 --> 00:40:25,520
and presented at command headquarters in a matter of seconds.

488
00:40:27,560 --> 00:40:32,520
SAGE was a multi-billion dollar research and development effort

489
00:40:32,521 --> 00:40:41,000
effort to create the first truly national-scale, networked, computerized information gathering

490
00:40:41,001 --> 00:40:48,670
and manipulation system. In many ways, it is the first model for the Internet.

491
00:40:48,671 --> 00:40:49,371
It was

492
00:40:49,372 --> 00:40:50,400
a remarkable achievement.

493
00:40:52,620 --> 00:40:59,300
Just as in World War II, the ever-changing dynamics of the Cold War propelled radar to

494
00:40:59,301 --> 00:41:01,540
ever greater heights of sophistication.

495
00:41:02,440 --> 00:41:07,000
When the Russian launch Sputnik ushered in the satellite age in 1957,

496
00:41:07,640 --> 00:41:11,200
radars began examining satellites for their military potential,

497
00:41:12,240 --> 00:41:14,700
a process that continues to this day.

498
00:41:16,320 --> 00:41:19,800
Modern radars have become pretty good at looking at an unknown satellite

499
00:41:19,801 --> 00:41:22,580
and determining its shape and its function.

500
00:41:23,020 --> 00:41:25,700
The details of how this is done are classified,

501
00:41:25,701 --> 00:41:29,160
but it's fair to say that we can produce an image of that satellite

502
00:41:29,161 --> 00:41:33,500
And, for example, if you had a Volkswagen or a Toyota in orbit,

503
00:41:33,820 --> 00:41:36,880
our modern radar technology could probably tell us which was which.

504
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:45,780
Beginning in the 1960s, intercontinental ballistic missiles offered radar a new challenge.

505
00:41:46,540 --> 00:41:52,460
An ICBM may carry many warheads, each one either a decoy or a live nuclear weapon.

506
00:41:52,461 --> 00:41:59,460
It costs an enormous amount of money to throw a 1,000 or 1,500 pound warhead intercontinental distances.

507
00:41:59,461 --> 00:42:04,460
The enemy tries to put all his money into the warheads and not much into the other stuff.

508
00:42:04,461 --> 00:42:08,460
And what we try and do is figure out what are the heavy bodies from the light bodies.

509
00:42:08,461 --> 00:42:16,460
So the problem simply boils down to reaching out 500 miles, tickling a body with a electromagnetic beam and trying to tell how much it weighs.

510
00:42:22,460 --> 00:42:36,460
radar has come a long way in the last 60 years from accidental echoes off passing ships

511
00:42:40,980 --> 00:42:44,600
to reflecting the wake of an earthbound ballistic missile

512
00:42:44,601 --> 00:42:50,980
60 years as a pivotal tool in the timeless battle between freedom and tyranny

513
00:42:52,460 --> 00:43:01,060
These cement foundations are all that remain today of the English Chain Home Network.

514
00:43:01,300 --> 00:43:05,860
They stand in mute tribute to the invention that won the Battle of Britain

515
00:43:05,861 --> 00:43:09,400
and rescued mankind from the abyss.

516
00:43:12,820 --> 00:43:18,560
The whole fury and might of the enemy must very soon be turned on us.

517
00:43:18,561 --> 00:43:24,600
Hitler knows that he will have to break us in this island or lose the war

518
00:43:24,601 --> 00:43:30,760
If we fail, then the whole world, including the United States

519
00:43:30,761 --> 00:43:33,900
Including all that we have known and cared for

520
00:43:33,901 --> 00:43:38,000
Will sink into the abyss of a new dark age

521
00:43:39,140 --> 00:43:43,580
Upon this battle depends the survival of Christian civilized nations

522
00:43:43,581 --> 00:43:53,380
But for radar's distant echoes,

523
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:56,240
Hitler might well have broken England.

524
00:43:57,140 --> 00:44:00,500
And with the loss of freedom's last outpost in Europe,

525
00:44:01,380 --> 00:44:04,540
history's tale could have been very different.

526
00:44:05,860 --> 00:44:08,860
Without radar, it would have made it very much more difficult

527
00:44:08,861 --> 00:44:10,940
to win the battle, but it was difficult enough.

528
00:44:13,580 --> 00:44:18,980
If that battle had been lost, my guess is that life would have been extremely unpleasant

529
00:44:18,981 --> 00:44:22,160
for those who lived in Great Britain.

530
00:44:22,161 --> 00:44:26,180
And we would never, ever have invaded the continent again because it was difficult enough

531
00:44:26,181 --> 00:44:27,820
invading it from this country.

532
00:44:27,821 --> 00:44:31,700
And had America come into the war to mount an invasion from America?

533
00:44:31,701 --> 00:44:32,700
No way.

534
00:44:32,701 --> 00:44:37,420
If that air war had been lost and the Germans had come here and had been successful, lots

535
00:44:37,421 --> 00:44:43,420
of ifs, but if it had happened, then, well, I wouldn't be here and you'd be speaking German.

536
00:44:43,421 --> 00:44:45,420
The FUERA!

537
00:44:45,421 --> 00:44:47,420
G!

